{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And it was when he descended, etc.</b> It says <b>וַיְהִי</b> in the language of distress, to allude to what befell all the righteous ones in the camp of Israel from the exaltedness of Moses which rebuked them to their faces, as it is written: ״וַיַּרְא אַהֲרֹן וְכָל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל אֶת מֹשֶׁה וְהִנֵּה קָרַן״ etc. (Exodus 34:30)." "<b>When Moses descended from the mountain, etc.</b> The purport of the verse is not known — where its place is. Further, one must know why he repeated saying <b>בְּרִדְתּוֹ מִן הָהָר</b> and was not satisfied with what he said nearby: ״בְּרֶדֶת מֹשֶׁה״ etc. Further, one must know for what purpose the verse informed us of Moses's lack of awareness in the matter, before it first informs us of the matter of the radiance of his face that exists. Further, one must know who informed Moses, since he did not know on his own; and if they informed him, why did the verse not inform us of the order of the informing?" "<b>Now,</b> the matter will be explained according to what the verse informs us after this — that Moses put a veil over his face continually, except at the time of his teaching. If so, one might say: why did Moses not put a veil over his face when he descended from the mountain, at a time when he was not teaching Torah to Israel? For this the verse says <b>וּמֹשֶׁה לֹא יָדַע</b> — this was the reason he did not put a veil over his face beforehand, and Aaron and Israel saw him, and behold, the skin of his face was radiant. But this still presents a difficulty: how could it be that a living person would not sense something additional upon his face, and the radiance of the face in particular? For this the verse preceded to state the reason that prevented him from knowing, and said <b>בְּרֶדֶת מֹשֶׁה מֵהַר</b> — meaning, for while he was on the mountain, even if he were to sense the glow of his face, he would attribute the matter to the fact that his face shone from the light of the face of El who spoke with him; and upon his descent from the mountain, that attribution of sensing the light of his face was removed. Yet it still remains difficult: why did he not sense it during the course of his descent? For this it says <b>וּשְׁנֵי לֻחוֹת הָעֵדֻת בְּיַד מֹשֶׁה בְּרִדְתּוֹ מִן הָהָר</b> — meaning, throughout the entire course of his descent from the mountain. And therefore he would attribute it thus: what he sensed in his face was from the light of the tablets, which shone opposite his face, and he did not know that a radiance of the face had become fixed in him in the skin of his face. And this is its saying <b>לֹא יָדַע כִּי קָרַן עוֹר פָּנָיו</b> — meaning, he knew of the radiance of his face, but he did not think that the light was fixed in the skin of his face, and he attributed it to the tablets that were in his hand; and this was the reason he did not make a veil, and Aaron saw him. And according to this, the purport of the verse is <b>וַיַּרְא אַהֲרֹן</b> etc., for without the entire verse Aaron and Israel would not have seen the radiance of the face before he called them, for he would have put on a veil as he did afterward, when he put the tablets down from his hand and there remained nothing to attribute it to — on his own he sensed the bestowal of Elohim that the skin of his face was radiant, and he immediately put on the veil, in the sod of (Proverbs 25:2) ״כְּבוֹד אֱלֹהִים הַסְתֵּר דָּבָר״." "<b>And in the Midrash</b> (Exodus Rabbah 47:6): Rabbi Yehudah bar Nachman said, when Moses wrote the Torah, a little remained in the reed, and he passed it over his head, and from there horns of splendor were made; end quote. And their words, of blessed memory, are sealed and closed. But perhaps they intended to say that we find that the trait of humility surpasses all, as they said, of blessed memory (Shir HaShirim Rabbah ch. 1): what Wisdom made as a crown for its head, which is the fear of YHWH, as it is written (Psalms 111:10): ״רֵאשִׁית חָכְמָה יִרְאַת ה׳״ — Humility made as a heel for its sandal, as it is written (Proverbs 22:4): ״עֵקֶב עֲנָוָה יִרְאַת ה׳״. And we find that the Torah testified about Moses (Numbers 12:3) that he was exceedingly humble beyond all men, and when HaShem said to write the matters, we find that he did not write עָנָיו but rather עָנָו — deficient of a yod, and even this was from humility. And as reward for this he merited horns of splendor, for he went to the extreme in his will, blessed be He; also HaShem went to the extreme in His kindness toward him by setting him apart from all men. And this is what Rabbi Yehudah alluded to in saying that a little remained in the reed — and it is the letter yod that he should have written and did not write on account of his abundant humility, and through it he merited as mentioned."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:58.927972544Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}